1. A quick check that Course 1 is still with you: update the paragraph so it reads exactly `Course 2 is interactive!`.

    ```html id=page2
    <p id="message">Course 2 starts here.</p>
    ```

    ```{javascript}
    const message = document.querySelector('#message');

    // Write your code here
    ```
    ```+id=page2
    ```
    ```+solution
    const message = document.querySelector('#message');

    message.innerText = 'Course 2 is interactive!';
    ```
    ```+test
    assert('#message').should('have.text', 'Course 2 is interactive!').run('The paragraph should read exactly `Course 2 is interactive!`', 'Updated the paragraph');
    ```

2. You have finished a practice problem and clicked "Mark as completed". Have you received credit for it?

    ```{mcq}
    Does marking a problem as completed submit it for grading?
    correct: Correct! "Mark as completed" is only a note to yourself. Credit comes from "Submit assignment", which sends your files to the automatic grader.
    wrong: Look back at "Submitting Assignments" and the difference between the two buttons.
    [x] No. You still have to click "Submit assignment" to be graded.
    [ ] Yes. Marking it completed runs the grader.
    [ ] Yes, but only for practice problems, not assessments.
    [ ] No, because practice problems are never graded at all.
    ```

3. Your submission comes back with failing tests. What is the most useful thing to look at?

    ```{mcq}
    The results pane says one test failed. Where do you look first?
    correct: Correct! The detailed feedback names what the grader was looking for and could not find, and the screenshots show the page it actually saw, which is usually the quickest route to the difference.
    wrong: Revisit the description of the test results pane and what scrolling down shows you.
    [x] The error message for the failing test, and the screenshots of the page the grader saw
    [ ] The total number of tests, and nothing else
    [ ] The "Mark as completed" button
    [ ] Nothing; resubmitting unchanged code usually fixes it
    ```

